## Subagent D — Adversarial benchmark/fairness reviewer

This subagent should inspect:

* the GraphLib architecture/review material;
* relevant Mathlib graph APIs;
* #503/#804/#805;
* the reports from Subagents A/B/C once available.

Its job is NOT to design an implementation.

Its job is to attack the benchmark protocol.

Look for hidden asymmetries such as:

* giving one side preprocessing for free and charging the other;
* forcing one side to use its mathematical graph directly;
* allowing one side an auxiliary adjacency representation but not the other;
* different theorem strengths;
* different loop or parallel-edge conventions;
* different definitions of `m`;
* counting edge occurrences differently;
* letting one side use an unrealistically constant-time `Finset` operation;
* hiding expensive reconstruction in a supposedly free abstraction;
* allowing one side to add graph-specific lemmas to its foundation while forbidding the other;
* counting generic data-structure deficiencies as graph-representation deficiencies;
* requiring one side to prove stronger representation correspondence;
* using asymptotic notation that hides materially different concrete costs;
* choosing a graph class that accidentally removes the central representation issue.

Classify every important potential bias as:

* blocking;
* should fix;
* acceptable but must be documented.

Deliver an adversarial fairness report to the main agent.

