# 10. Foundation modification policy

For the later benchmark, neither side should be allowed to silently grow its underlying graph foundation until the benchmark becomes easy.

Prefer:

* no modifications to the underlying GraphLib or Mathlib graph foundation during the first implementation attempt;
* benchmark-local helper definitions and lemmas are allowed;
* any missing foundation API should be recorded explicitly.

If a tiny foundation repair is genuinely necessary, record:

* exact change;
* why it was necessary;
* whether an analogous change would have been allowed on the other side.

The comparison should distinguish:

1. existing-library support;
2. benchmark-local bridge code;
3. proposed upstream/foundation improvements.

---

# 11. Cost-model realism

The protocol must not allow statements such as:

> “membership/update costs one tick”

unless the chosen framework makes clear what this means and why it corresponds to a defensible algorithmic cost model.

In particular, investigate and freeze treatment of:

* array reads/writes;
* persistent versus mutable arrays;
* stack push/pop;
* cursor increments;
* edge-used tests/updates;
* map/set lookup;
* `Finset` membership/insertion;
* list scanning;
* finite enumeration;
* loop overhead;
* preprocessing;
* allocation.

Do not accidentally treat a linear-time `Finset` lookup as unit-cost merely because it is written as one Lean expression.

If the cost model abstracts over a data structure operation, make the abstraction explicit.

If the model assumes a RAM primitive, say so.

If the theorem is a manually annotated resource count rather than a machine-runtime theorem, say so.

